An occluded window keeps its page's compositor and last frame (#662), for a while. A window whose Space goes out of
view is occluded, and after Chrome's occlusion delay `RenderWidgetHostViewMac::WasOccluded` hides the host, so
`BrowserCompositorMac::UpdateState` put the page's own compositor up for recycling: the NSView lost its layer
contents, and on return the page stayed blank until the renderer drew again (2-3 s in reports). Chrome's own pages
use their parent layer's compositor and keep their frame when only occluded; RayCore's page views own theirs. Now
while the host is hidden but the view's root layer is still visible (occluded, not hidden), the compositor stays
(`BrowserCompositorMac::HoldForOcclusion`), and showing the host again re-shows the DelegatedFrameHost
(`BrowserCompositorMac::ShowHeld`). A real hide (the view made invisible) still retires the compositor as before.

The hold is bounded, because each held compositor keeps one frame's IOSurface in the GPU process (measured 10-30 MB
a window: 11.8 -> 24.4 MB for a static page, 13.1 -> 42.3 MB for a 720p video). After 10 minutes occluded
(`hold_for` of the `HoldCompositorWhileOccluded` feature) the compositor is retired as before the hold
(`BrowserCompositorMac::ReleaseHeld`), so a window left on another Space costs nothing after that and returns blank
until the renderer draws, as without the hold. 10 minutes covers going to another Space and back in the same task,
which is what the hold is for, while a window left behind for longer stops costing memory. Under memory pressure no
compositor is held and every held one is released (a `base::MemoryConsumer`, which the browser's
`MemoryPressureListenerPolicy` drives from the system's pressure level and back when it ends). The timer stops when the host is shown again, on any real state change and on destruction.
`--enable-features=HoldCompositorWhileOccluded:hold_for/5s` shortens the hold for tests;
`--disable-features=HoldCompositorWhileOccluded` turns it off.

